---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../components/work/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import CaseColophon from '../../components/work/CaseColophon.astro';
import PermittingDiagram from '../../components/work/diagrams/PermittingDiagram.astro';

const facts = [
  { label: 'CLIENT', value: 'Florida DOH · Manatee County (via Troubadour Health)' },
  { label: 'SECTOR', value: 'Government · Environmental public health' },
  { label: 'ROLE', value: 'Primary responsibility · system architecture and implementation' },
  { label: 'TIMELINE', value: 'Spring to Summer 2026' },
  { label: 'STATUS', value: 'Built, validated, handed off', accent: true },
];
---

<Base
  title="Florida DOH permit modernization · Michael Doerffler"
  description="Case study: rebuilding a county health department's pool permitting into a single automated system of record, and giving the department data it could manage the work with for the first time."
>
  <CaseStudyLayout
    caseLabel="FLORIDA DOH · PERMIT MODERNIZATION"
    eyebrow="GOVERNMENT · PROCESS AUTOMATION"
    titleLine1="From a shared inbox"
    titleLine2="to a system of record."
    standfirst="A county health department's pool permitting modernization had stalled. I picked it up, rebuilt the program as a single automated system of record, and gave the department something it never had: data it could actually manage the work with."
    nextLabel="A-LIGN"
    nextHref="/work/prospecting-automation"
  >
    <CaseColophon items={facts} />

    <section class="case-section">
      <div class="sec-label" data-reveal>THE CHALLENGE</div>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        More than fifty pool permits a year arrived by email. Details were retyped into a
        spreadsheet, status was tracked by inbox and memory, and every letter was typed by hand.
        Applications routinely arrived incomplete, and nobody knew until someone opened them.
      </p>
      <p class="situation-stmt" data-reveal>
        But the deeper problem wasn't the manual labor at all. It was that the department
        <span class="situation-key">couldn't see its own work</span>.
      </p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        Status lived in four places at once and none of them agreed, which meant leadership could not
        answer a single basic management question:
      </p>
      <ul class="questions" data-reveal>
        <li>How long does a permit actually take?</li>
        <li>Where do they stall?</li>
        <li>Is the statutory thirty-day review clock being met?</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        Those answers weren't hard to get. They were structurally impossible, because the data didn't
        exist in any form you could ask a question of.
      </p>
      <p class="body-muted" data-reveal>
        An earlier internal effort never delivered. When the project was revived with an outside
        consultant, the technical lead was scoped to sit on the DOH side. The build's complexity
        exceeded that arrangement and it was reassigned. That's where I picked it up.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section case-exhibit">
      <PermittingDiagram />
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <div class="sec-label" data-reveal>WHAT GOT BUILT</div>
      <p class="situation-stmt" data-reveal>One intake, one record, seven flows.</p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        The form is designed against two things at once: the Excel sheet staff already lived in, and
        the DH 4159 state form. Matching both meant the new system produced records staff would
        recognize and the state would accept. The SharePoint list behind it is the program's first
        real system of record, with fifty-one historical records migrated in and status values
        rebuilt to match the milestones the manager already used rather than generic labels.
      </p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        A version of this system already existed in another county, built for septic permits.
        Copying it was the fast path and the wrong one. Most of the early rework came from the gap
        between what pools actually need and what septic had been built for.
      </p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        Much of the calendar went to the parts you can't see: getting access provisioned inside a
        government tenant, and getting the consultant, the department, and government IT aligned on
        who could touch what. Once both were in place, the core system came together in a few weeks.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <div class="sec-label" data-reveal>RIGHT-SIZING AGAINST REALITY</div>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        I planned for more than I built. The original design called for heavier automation and a
        much larger list.
      </p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        I pulled back, for reasons about the client rather than the code. The team maintaining it is
        small, there is no dedicated technical staff to fix it when something breaks, and this was
        an active production project in a government tenant.
      </p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        The clearest case is letters. I built three Word templates with content controls and then
        didn't ship them. Part of that was fragility, since a letter flow that fails quietly means a
        permit notice that never goes out. The better reason is that this is a regulatory program,
        and the system shouldn't write to an applicant on behalf of staff. It sends exactly one
        thing on its own: the confirmation that a submission was received. Everything else is a
        reminder telling a person it's their turn. Staff stayed the decision-makers.
      </p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        Every other cut went the same way. The EPH manager who has run this program for years and I
        took the list from roughly ninety columns to thirty-five, one field at a time. Does this
        field earn its place, or is it a column nobody will ever fill?
      </p>
      <p class="closing-note" data-reveal>
        What got built is smaller than what was drawn. That was the point.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section quote-band" data-reveal>
      <div class="sec-label quote-band-label">FROM THE ENGAGEMENT LEAD</div>
      <blockquote class="client-quote">
        <p class="cq-text">
          "The technical requirements ultimately exceeded the initial scope of the engagement and
          those typically expected of an internship. Michael effectively assumed primary
          responsibility for the system architecture and implementation."
        </p>
        <footer class="cq-attr">
          Joshua T. Barnett, PhD
          <span class="cq-role">CEO, Troubadour Health</span>
        </footer>
      </blockquote>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <div class="sec-label" data-reveal>THE IMPACT</div>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        One centralized record in place of four disagreeing copies. Every permit timestamped and
        moving through defined milestones. Work that ran on staff memory now runs on its own, and
        incomplete applications are rejected at intake. The whole thing runs on Microsoft 365
        licensing the department already owned, which is the reason they can actually keep it.
      </p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        The system was handed off in July 2026, so it hasn't produced a cycle-time number yet.
        That's the point. The first thing it generates is the baseline that never existed: volume,
        cycle time, where permits stall, and whether the thirty-day clock is being met.
      </p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        Pools is phase one. The same architecture extends to food service, lodging, groupcare, and
        septic without rebuilding each time.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <div class="sec-label" data-reveal>HOW IT CLOSED</div>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        I delivered a leadership summary and a staff guide, then ran a live walkthrough to train the
        team. The EPH manager, the consultant, and the IT team were genuinely excited to see the
        system come to life, and to get their time back from the repetitive manual work it replaces.
      </p>
      <p class="closing-note" data-reveal>
        The difference between a system that gets used and one that gets abandoned is almost
        entirely what happens in the last week.
      </p>
    </section>
  </CaseStudyLayout>
</Base>

<style>
  .case-section { margin-top: 9vh; }
  .case-exhibit { margin-top: 6vh; }

  /* Big display statement (the pivot / lead lines) */
  .situation-stmt {
    margin: 5vh 0 0;
    max-width: 1050px;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(22px, 2.4vw, 36px);
    line-height: 1.35;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .situation-key {
    color: var(--accent);
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--accent);
  }

  /* Standard body copy */
  .body {
    margin: 3.5vh 0 0;
    max-width: 900px;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: clamp(16px, 1.3vw, 18px);
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--ink-muted-2);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .body-muted {
    margin: 4vh 0 0;
    max-width: 880px;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: clamp(14px, 1.1vw, 16px);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink-muted-4);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* The unanswerable management questions */
  .questions {
    margin: 4vh 0 0;
    max-width: 900px;
    padding: 2px 0 2px 26px;
    border-left: 2px solid rgba(46, 74, 58, 0.35);
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 13px;
  }
  .questions li {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(17px, 1.5vw, 22px);
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--ink-muted-2);
  }

  /* Closing statement */
  .closing-note {
    margin: 5vh 0 0;
    max-width: 820px;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(20px, 2vw, 30px);
    line-height: 1.35;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* Client pull-quote: green panel */
  .quote-band {
    background: rgba(46, 74, 58, 0.88);
    border-radius: 14px;
    padding: 4.5vh 3.5vw;
    max-width: 1000px;
  }
  .quote-band .quote-band-label { color: rgba(233, 225, 207, 0.75); }
  .client-quote {
    margin: 2.5vh 0 0;
    max-width: 820px;
  }
  .cq-text {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.3vw, 18px);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--bone);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .cq-attr {
    margin-top: 2.5vh;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 4px;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: var(--bone);
  }
  .cq-role { color: rgba(233, 225, 207, 0.6); font-weight: 400; }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .case-section { margin-top: 7vh; }
  }
</style>
